    description:
      "The consensus recommendation for training and competing in the heat is to acclimatise with repeated exercise-heat exposures over 1–2 weeks, start euhydrated, and use pre-cooling where available. A meta-analysis of 96 articles found heat adaptation had a moderate beneficial effect on exercise capacity and performance in the heat, lowered core temperature and improved cardiovascular stability, with regimens longer than 14 days most effective. In 12 trained cyclists, a 10-day acclimation program raised VO2max by 5% in cool and 8% in hot conditions and time-trial performance by 6% and 8%, with plasma-volume expansion; the boundary is that this cross-over benefit in the cool has not been consistently replicated and adaptations decay within weeks.",
    action: "In the two weeks before a hot-weather race, do five to ten 60–90 minute sessions at moderate intensity in the heat or with added clothing, weigh yourself before and after to replace fluid, and finish the block 3–5 days before competing.",
